115
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:02,300
it gets sent again

116
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:03,640
when the second tool call is completed

117
00:04:03,640 --> 00:04:04,500
it gets sent again

118
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:05,980
when you send a new prompt

119
00:04:05,980 --> 00:04:06,920
it will still be sent

120
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:08,000
every tool invocation

121
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,420
the entire History will be resent every time

122
00:04:10,420 --> 00:04:11,640
If you think about it

123
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:12,480
That actually makes sense

124
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:14,250
because there are agent calls here

125
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:15,090
Got it

126
00:04:15,090 --> 00:04:17,170
First, let's gain a better understanding of the codebase

127
00:04:17,170 --> 00:04:18,730
then it outputs a tool

128
00:04:18,730 --> 00:04:19,769
calling the agent

129
00:04:19,769 --> 00:04:21,300
It's actually just auto-completing

130
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:22,940
It is merely generating text

131
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:24,880
It has no idea what's happening

132
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:26,480
He doesn't know the system behind it

133
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:28,090
nor does it know how much time it will take

134
00:04:28,090 --> 00:04:30,730
People seem to think the system pauses during tool calls

135
00:04:30,730 --> 00:04:32,380
But that's not the case

136
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:34,860
A tool call indicates the generation process has concluded

137
00:04:34,860 --> 00:04:36,820
The AI is not running Alpha at this moment

138
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:37,980
When a tool call occurs

139
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:39,000
The AI pauses

140
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:40,680
In fact, it's as if it had died

141
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:41,540
terminated the process

142
00:04:41,540 --> 00:04:42,160
closed

143
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:43,870
When the tool call is completed

144
00:04:43,870 --> 00:04:47,390
All content from the beginning to this point will be resent to the AI

145
00:04:47,390 --> 00:04:49,840
Send it back to the service running it via API calls

146
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:51,840
Now it has the new History

147
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:54,330
and continues generation based on this

148
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:54,890
Someone asked

149
00:04:54,890 --> 00:04:56,570
Is this to verify whether the tool call was successful?

150
00:04:56,570 --> 00:04:58,690
No, this is to retrieve the return information from the tool call

151
00:04:58,690 --> 00:05:00,549
The tool call is to list the project directory

152
00:05:00,549 --> 00:05:01,669
That was an iOS call

153
00:05:01,669 --> 00:05:03,109
So now we have the output result

154
00:05:03,109 --> 00:05:04,449
but before getting the output

155
00:05:04,449 --> 00:05:05,369
it can do nothing

156
00:05:05,369 --> 00:05:06,590
so it stopped

157
00:05:06,590 --> 00:05:08,310
Therefore, every time a tool call occurs

158
00:05:08,310 --> 00:05:10,610
the model needs to know the result of the tool call

159
00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:12,220
so once the tool call is completed

160
00:05:12,220 --> 00:05:13,560
it sends everything back

161
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:14,820
because it requires complete information

162
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:17,340
This is just like the plot of Groundhog Day for the model

163
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:18,060
every time it wakes up

164
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,320
There's nothing left in the brain

165
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:20,340
It is stateless

166
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:21,590
it retains nothing

167
00:05:21,590 --> 00:05:24,380
so every time it has to transfer all the states back

168
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:27,740
So if you're running an AI agent for long-running tasks

169
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:28,960
monitor network traffic

170
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:29,580
will find

171
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:33,020
every tool call is sending the entire History back to

172
00:05:33,020 --> 00:05:34,380
Anthropic api

173
00:05:34,380 --> 00:05:37,620
OpenAI API or any other interface

174
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:40,960
then any available GPU will receive all this context

175
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:41,780
and load it

176
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:42,950
generated the next response

177
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:45,290
I know what you're thinking, hmm

178
00:05:45,290 --> 00:05:46,690
Isn't that what download is for?

179
00:05:46,690 --> 00:05:49,190
Download is only meant to reduce the computations the model needs to perform.

180
00:05:49,190 --> 00:05:52,660
This makes generating the first token cheaper and faster

181
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:55,260
but it does not change the amount of data you need to transmit

182
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:58,380
because the key for the download is the hash value of your History

183
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:00,740
so even if this part of the content has been cached

184
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:04,400
you're not telling the AI 'hey, fetch the data from the download'

185
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:05,820
and then add new content

186
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:07,980
you are still sending the complete History

187
00:06:07,980 --> 00:06:09,380
then it performs hash computation

188
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:10,640
and say oh crap

189
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:12,860
We have downloaded the history up to this point

190
00:06:12,860 --> 00:06:14,420
This is the new part

191
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:17,940
so we will load the state up to that new content

192
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:19,060
then add the new content

193
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:20,580
then start generating the response

194
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:22,160
This is the role of download

195
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:24,540
Download does not change the amount of data you need to send

196
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:28,080
Download only changes the time required to process the data

197
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:29,800
It's essentially just a hash value

198
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,440
So download is just a method to reduce computation

199
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:34,440
It does not change the traffic that must be transmitted at all

200
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,460
I want to make this extremely, extremely clear

201
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:40,260
Someone asked if this is compression No compression is very different

202
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:42,080
Compression is completely different

203
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:44,380
Compression refers to fetching the entire History

204
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:45,720
and summarize it

205
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:47,960
so you no longer need all the tokens

206
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:49,720
But compression breaks the download

207
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:51,080
It doesn't help with download

208
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:53,100
Compression means choosing to forgo download

209
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:55,800
because you originally wanted a shorter History

210
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:58,700
Hopefully you can now somewhat see where the problem lies.

211
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:02,150
The problem is not that the model has difficulty obtaining this data

212
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:03,010
Simply put

213
00:07:03,010 --> 00:07:06,440
upload all content every time a tool is called

214
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:08,680
and the data volume keeps increasing each time

215
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:10,740
This really sucks

216
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:13,660
This is really inefficient

217
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:14,960
This is really expensive

218
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:17,039
This consumes a significant amount of bandwidth

219
00:07:17,039 --> 00:07:19,319
This means the cost of running multiple AI agents in parallel

220
00:07:19,319 --> 00:07:20,399
higher than it should be

221
00:07:20,399 --> 00:07:21,139
This means

222
00:07:21,139 --> 00:07:24,879
OpenAI's API needs to be able to handle these massive text payloads

223
00:07:24,879 --> 00:07:26,580
and all matters related to routing

224
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:28,960
If you don't need to send all this content

225
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:30,720
and only need to send the new content

226
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:32,240
namely tool invocation

227
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:33,480
Wouldn't that be much easier?

228
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:34,750
Isn't that pretty cool?

229
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:36,470
but this will complicate things

230
00:07:36,470 --> 00:07:40,159
Because in reality, OpenAI's infrastructure doesn't support this approach

231
00:07:40,159 --> 00:07:41,919
OpenAI does have a lot of GPUs

232
00:07:41,919 --> 00:07:43,819
but you're not directly accessing these GPUs

233
00:07:43,819 --> 00:07:46,059
Obviously you're not sending requests directly to the GPU

234
00:07:46,059 --> 00:07:48,299
because then we would have to completely format everything in a different way

235
00:07:48,299 --> 00:07:49,510
format all the content

236
00:07:49,510 --> 00:07:50,430
that makes no sense

237
00:07:50,430 --> 00:07:53,340
and that particular GPU may not have your specific download

238
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:55,460
so in fact all these requests

239
00:07:55,460 --> 00:07:57,470
must pass through an orchestration layer

240
00:07:57,470 --> 00:08:01,490
which means there is this API in front of OpenAI's GPUs

241
00:08:01,490 --> 00:08:03,800
Of course, they possess far more than just a single graphics card

242
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,100
they have thousands of GPUs

243
00:08:06,100 --> 00:08:07,840
distributed across many different locations

244
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:09,659
So your request first enters the API

245
00:08:09,659 --> 00:08:11,219
Once the request enters the API

246
00:08:11,219 --> 00:08:12,159
It will perform checks

247
00:08:12,159 --> 00:08:13,079
it checks

248
00:08:13,079 --> 00:08:16,350
for example, whether there is a download value at our previous location

249
00:08:16,350 --> 00:08:18,950
Do we have any free GPUs to route this request to

250
00:08:18,950 --> 00:08:21,390
Does the user have permission to perform this operation

251
00:08:21,390 --> 00:08:24,840
Then he routes the resulting output to a specific GPU

252
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:26,060
If all goes well

253
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:27,620
he will return those tokens

254
00:08:27,620 --> 00:08:29,880
These tokens will then be sent back to you

255
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:32,190
eventually becomes the AI agent's response

256
00:08:32,190 --> 00:08:33,289
but also remember

257
00:08:33,289 --> 00:08:35,490
The API is not as simple as it seems

258
00:08:35,490 --> 00:08:36,110
Look here

259
00:08:36,110 --> 00:08:38,549
There are many independent API nodes here

260
00:08:38,549 --> 00:08:42,929
they are all searching for randomly available GPUs and performing routing

261
00:08:43,250 --> 00:08:44,650
so your first request

262
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:46,310
may enter the first API node

263
00:08:46,310 --> 00:08:48,730
The second request might reach the fourth API node

264
00:08:48,730 --> 00:08:52,080
and this request may end up on a completely different GPU

265
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:53,660
Do you see the problem here?

266
00:08:53,660 --> 00:08:56,140
even if the server wants to maintain this state

267
00:08:56,140 --> 00:08:57,740
to ensure the correct node

268
00:08:57,740 --> 00:08:59,300
have the correct context

269
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:00,730
is also an annoying matter

270
00:09:00,730 --> 00:09:03,810
I'm certain the download layer is completely independent of the other two.

271
00:09:03,810 --> 00:09:05,820
The third layer needs to manage all these tasks

272
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:07,180
So when you send a request

273
00:09:07,180 --> 00:09:10,080
It will enter a server operated by OpenAI

274
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:12,800
This is a type of server they have in large quantities that's relatively dumb

275
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:14,480
it has to check which download

276
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:16,340
may contain the cached value corresponding to your token

277
00:09:16,340 --> 00:09:19,460
